1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,474
What if I stopped waiting for the perfect
idea?

2
00:00:03,569 --> 00:00:04,942
The perfect software.

3
00:00:04,985 --> 00:00:06,227
The perfect moment.

4
00:00:06,266 --> 00:00:07,770
What if I just started?

5
00:00:07,816 --> 00:00:08,797
I already have.

6
00:00:08,828 --> 00:00:11,313
I've begun reaching out to businesses,

7
00:00:15,165 --> 00:00:19,939
so far, and I'm trying to turn those
conversations into paying customers.

8
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:23,749
But there's a second experiment hiding
inside the first.

9
00:00:23,862 --> 00:00:27,394
I'm also building the software I use to
run all of it.

10
00:00:27,503 --> 00:00:28,353
The pipeline.

11
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,361
The follow-ups.

12
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:30,307
The playbooks.

13
00:00:30,335 --> 00:00:34,847
Every step I take, tracked in a tool that
I'm writing while I use it.

14
00:00:34,987 --> 00:00:39,565
If it works, I could end up with two
businesses out of one experiment.

15
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:43,042
If it doesn't, you'll get to watch me find
out why.

16
00:00:43,145 --> 00:00:43,864
I'm Andrew.

17
00:00:43,886 --> 00:00:46,241
This is the Bootstrapper Experiment.

18
00:00:46,314 --> 00:00:47,098
Episode one.

19
00:00:48,023 --> 00:00:50,538
Quick background, and I'll keep it short.

20
00:00:50,615 --> 00:00:53,499
I've spent years in sales and customer
service.

21
00:00:53,588 --> 00:00:57,759
I've run my own businesses, and I've
helped other people run theirs.

22
00:01:01,429 --> 00:01:05,048
These days, a lot of what I do is use AI
to build websites,

23
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:08,411
funnels and small apps that solve a
specific problem.

24
00:01:08,512 --> 00:01:11,088
Here's what I've learned from all of that.

25
00:01:11,168 --> 00:01:13,008
Building is the easy part now.

26
00:01:13,065 --> 00:01:17,788
The hard part is the thing nobody films:
getting the first people to say yes.

27
00:01:17,935 --> 00:01:20,143
I'm not saying that to sound clever.

28
00:01:20,211 --> 00:01:24,137
I can get a working page or app live much
faster than I used to.

29
00:01:24,258 --> 00:01:28,062
Getting someone to care about it hasn't
got any faster at all.

30
00:01:28,179 --> 00:01:32,964
About two weeks ago I built a system that
went out and found opportunities for

31
00:01:33,112 --> 00:01:33,602
me.

32
00:01:33,618 --> 00:01:36,623
It found over a hundred that it called
qualified.

33
00:01:36,716 --> 00:01:39,599
Verified revenue from that system, at the
time?

34
00:01:39,689 --> 00:01:40,179
Zero.

35
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:44,120
Those two numbers, side by side, taught me
more than any course.

36
00:01:44,242 --> 00:01:46,143
A big list looks like progress.

37
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:46,754
It isn't.

38
00:01:46,771 --> 00:01:48,612
So I'm doing this differently.

39
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:57,178
And I'm doing it in public, because a story
with only the wins in it isn't useful

40
00:01:57,332 --> 00:01:57,946
to anyone.

41
00:01:57,964 --> 00:01:59,375
You'll see the numbers.

42
00:01:59,419 --> 00:02:00,830
Including the bad ones.

43
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:03,397
So what's the actual business?

44
00:02:06,570 --> 00:02:07,490
The customers are

45
00:02:10,642 --> 00:02:11,670
How do I find them?

46
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:13,108
Not the way I first tried.

47
00:02:13,152 --> 00:02:17,372
I tested a proper business-contact
database, and it simply doesn't cover small

48
00:02:17,502 --> 00:02:18,205
local trades.

49
00:02:18,227 --> 00:02:22,068
So I'm starting where the real
conversations are: businesses I can see,

50
00:02:22,187 --> 00:02:24,838
people I know, and people I can be
introduced to.

51
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,814
The plan from the start was simple.

52
00:02:26,872 --> 00:02:30,389
Pick a small group of real contacts, send
a real message to each,

53
00:02:30,498 --> 00:02:31,742
and watch what happens.

54
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:32,971
Not a thousand emails.

55
00:02:33,008 --> 00:02:34,468
Ten or fifteen honest ones.

56
00:02:34,513 --> 00:02:37,705
I know ten or fifteen conversations won't
prove a business.

57
00:02:37,804 --> 00:02:39,049
They're a first sample.

58
00:02:39,087 --> 00:02:42,063
What they will tell me is whether people
answer at all,

59
00:02:42,155 --> 00:02:44,697
which words get a reply, and where I get
stuck.

60
00:02:44,776 --> 00:02:47,157
Right now that's worth more than a big
list.

61
00:02:50,354 --> 00:02:52,247
I want to be clear about one thing.

62
00:02:52,306 --> 00:02:53,225
As I record this,

63
00:02:56,377 --> 00:02:56,810
.

64
00:02:56,823 --> 00:03:00,070
When that changes, you'll see the proof,
not just the claim.

65
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:05,146
Now the software, because this is where it
gets interesting.

66
00:03:05,272 --> 00:03:09,279
When you do outreach, the work isn't just
sending messages.

67
00:03:09,403 --> 00:03:10,558
It's remembering.

68
00:03:10,594 --> 00:03:11,816
Who did I contact?

69
00:03:11,854 --> 00:03:12,873
What did I say?

70
00:03:12,904 --> 00:03:13,923
Did they reply?

71
00:03:13,955 --> 00:03:15,585
When should I follow up?

72
00:03:15,636 --> 00:03:17,673
Which message actually worked?

73
00:03:17,736 --> 00:03:22,084
If that lives in your head, or scattered
across notes and chats,

74
00:03:22,218 --> 00:03:24,460
you can't learn anything from it.

75
00:03:24,529 --> 00:03:26,838
You just repeat yourself and hope.

76
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:29,491
So I'm building the tool I wish I had.

77
00:03:29,571 --> 00:03:33,171
It has a Today screen that tells me what
to do first:

78
00:03:33,282 --> 00:03:37,630
who's due for a follow-up, who replied,
and who's going nowhere.

79
00:03:37,764 --> 00:03:41,772
It has a pipeline, so every business sits
at a clear stage,

80
00:03:41,896 --> 00:03:44,477
from just found, to customer, or lost.

81
00:03:44,557 --> 00:03:45,983
And it has playbooks.

82
00:03:46,027 --> 00:03:51,733
Every task has a priority and a due date,
and every task points back to the business

83
00:03:51,909 --> 00:03:52,860
it belongs to.

84
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:54,995
So Today isn't a wall of notes.

85
00:03:55,061 --> 00:03:57,778
It's a short list I can actually finish.

86
00:03:57,862 --> 00:04:03,024
I set daily targets, like five prospects
researched and five first messages,

87
00:04:03,184 --> 00:04:06,852
and I can change them whenever they stop
making sense.

88
00:04:06,965 --> 00:04:09,750
A playbook is a set of steps for one job.

89
00:04:09,836 --> 00:04:11,398
Researching a prospect.

90
00:04:11,447 --> 00:04:13,077
Writing a first message.

91
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:14,010
Following up.

92
00:04:14,038 --> 00:04:15,532
Handling an objection.

93
00:04:15,578 --> 00:04:19,586
There are twelve to start with, and
they're fully editable,

94
00:04:19,710 --> 00:04:22,223
because my offer won't be your offer.

95
00:04:22,301 --> 00:04:24,203
Everything I do gets logged.

96
00:04:24,262 --> 00:04:27,998
Open any business and you can step through
its history,

97
00:04:28,113 --> 00:04:31,170
event by event, to see exactly what
happened.

98
00:04:31,264 --> 00:04:33,370
Nothing gets quietly rewritten.

99
00:04:33,435 --> 00:04:36,152
And the reports are deliberately honest.

100
00:04:36,236 --> 00:04:40,787
If I've sent six messages and one got a
reply, it shows one of six.

101
00:04:40,928 --> 00:04:42,422
Not seventeen percent.

102
00:04:42,469 --> 00:04:44,710
Six isn't enough to say anything.

103
00:04:44,779 --> 00:04:48,312
What it doesn't do: it doesn't send
messages for me,

104
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:50,798
and it doesn't pretend to be smart.

105
00:04:50,872 --> 00:04:52,842
There's no AI judge in there.

106
00:04:52,903 --> 00:04:56,571
It records what I do, and it shows me what
I recorded.

107
00:04:56,684 --> 00:05:00,895
I've tested the whole journey, from first
note to closed deal,

108
00:05:01,026 --> 00:05:03,471
in a browser, on made-up businesses.

109
00:05:08,368 --> 00:05:09,959
Planning is comfortable.

110
00:05:10,008 --> 00:05:12,129
Execution is where it gets real.

111
00:05:12,194 --> 00:05:14,713
Here's how a working session goes now.

112
00:05:14,791 --> 00:05:16,381
I open the Today screen.

113
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:17,756
It shows what's due.

114
00:05:17,797 --> 00:05:20,580
I pick a business and start with research.

115
00:05:20,666 --> 00:05:22,058
Ten minutes, no more.

116
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:25,614
I write down one specific thing I noticed
about them,

117
00:05:25,722 --> 00:05:27,578
something true and relevant.

118
00:05:27,635 --> 00:05:31,943
That's what keeps the message from
sounding like everyone else's.

119
00:05:32,076 --> 00:05:35,191
Then I decide whether they're worth
contacting.

120
00:05:35,288 --> 00:05:36,878
Qualified, maybe, or no.

121
00:05:36,927 --> 00:05:38,584
Writing no down is a win.

122
00:05:38,636 --> 00:05:39,762
It saves me time.

123
00:05:39,797 --> 00:05:42,117
I'm trying to be strict about this.

124
00:05:42,188 --> 00:05:47,490
A business I like the idea of isn't the
same as a business that actually has the

125
00:05:47,654 --> 00:05:48,715
problem I solve.

126
00:05:48,748 --> 00:05:52,127
If it's a yes, I follow the first-message
playbook.

127
00:05:52,232 --> 00:05:53,027
One channel.

128
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:54,245
One short message.

129
00:05:54,282 --> 00:05:55,541
One small question.

130
00:05:55,580 --> 00:06:00,219
I give the approach a name, so I can
compare it with the others later.

131
00:06:00,363 --> 00:06:03,743
Then comes the part that's genuinely
uncomfortable:

132
00:06:03,847 --> 00:06:04,775
pressing send.

133
00:06:08,630 --> 00:06:13,799
After I send it, I log it, and I set a
follow-up date before I close the form.

134
00:06:13,959 --> 00:06:16,411
That small habit is the whole system.

135
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:20,331
A message with no follow-up date is a
message I'll forget.

136
00:06:24,276 --> 00:06:28,981
And when someone replies, I record it, and
the tool moves them forward.

137
00:06:29,127 --> 00:06:29,790
A meeting.

138
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:30,539
A proposal.

139
00:06:30,562 --> 00:06:31,490
A yes or a no.

140
00:06:31,518 --> 00:06:32,976
Each step gets a date.

141
00:06:33,021 --> 00:06:36,269
The numbers that matter right now aren't
revenue.

142
00:06:36,369 --> 00:06:37,430
They're simpler.

143
00:06:37,462 --> 00:06:39,583
How many real messages went out.

144
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:41,968
How many follow-ups I actually did.

145
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:43,631
How many people replied.

146
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:45,204
None of this is clever.

147
00:06:45,251 --> 00:06:46,378
That's the point.

148
00:06:46,413 --> 00:06:49,064
It's a way to turn effort into evidence.

149
00:06:50,046 --> 00:06:51,842
Here's the bigger question.

150
00:06:51,898 --> 00:06:54,093
Could this software be a product?

151
00:06:54,161 --> 00:06:58,220
I think there's a version of it that could
help other people.

152
00:06:58,345 --> 00:06:59,144
Freelancers.

153
00:06:59,168 --> 00:07:00,166
Small agencies.

154
00:07:00,197 --> 00:07:05,387
Anyone building a business through outreach
who needs a way to know what to do

155
00:07:05,547 --> 00:07:07,743
today, and what worked last week.

156
00:07:07,811 --> 00:07:09,474
But I want to be careful.

157
00:07:09,525 --> 00:07:12,054
Right now I have exactly one user: me.

158
00:07:12,132 --> 00:07:14,261
Zero customers for the software.

159
00:07:14,327 --> 00:07:16,589
I'm not claiming anybody wants it.

160
00:07:16,659 --> 00:07:18,389
I haven't proven anything.

161
00:07:18,442 --> 00:07:20,505
What I do have is an advantage.

162
00:07:20,568 --> 00:07:24,228
I'm building it from a problem I'm living
in real time.

163
00:07:24,341 --> 00:07:29,065
Every feature exists because I either
needed it, or found out I didn't.

164
00:07:29,211 --> 00:07:34,600
A tool like this would have to be simple
enough that one person can use it in ten

165
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:39,490
minutes a day, and solid enough that a
small team could share it later.

166
00:07:39,636 --> 00:07:40,834
That's the target.

167
00:07:40,871 --> 00:07:43,732
I'm a long way from knowing if I've hit
it.

168
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:45,084
So here's the rule.

169
00:07:45,123 --> 00:07:48,849
The software has to earn its place in my
own week first.

170
00:07:48,965 --> 00:07:54,620
If I keep using it when I'm busy, and it keeps
showing me things I would have missed,

171
00:07:54,795 --> 00:07:55,859
that's a signal.

172
00:07:55,892 --> 00:07:59,951
If I stop opening it, that's a signal too,
and I'll tell you.

173
00:08:00,076 --> 00:08:04,999
Only then does it make sense to ask
whether someone else would pay for it.

174
00:08:05,152 --> 00:08:07,214
Two businesses, one experiment.

175
00:08:07,278 --> 00:08:10,871
But the second one only exists if the
first one works.

176
00:08:11,882 --> 00:08:13,163
So what happens next?

177
00:08:13,203 --> 00:08:14,484
Here's the challenge.

178
00:08:18,046 --> 00:08:19,815
Here's what I don't know yet.

179
00:08:19,870 --> 00:08:21,944
Whether my offer is the right one.

180
00:08:22,008 --> 00:08:23,594
Whether my price is right.

181
00:08:23,643 --> 00:08:26,877
Whether the people I'm contacting will
answer at all.

182
00:08:26,977 --> 00:08:31,247
And whether this software is something
I'll still be using in a month.

183
00:08:31,379 --> 00:08:35,406
I'd rather say that out loud than pretend
I've got it figured out.

184
00:08:35,531 --> 00:08:36,263
Why film it?

185
00:08:36,285 --> 00:08:39,946
Because there's a version of starting that
nobody shows you.

186
00:08:40,059 --> 00:08:41,523
Not the polished launch.

187
00:08:41,569 --> 00:08:44,863
The Tuesday where you send a message and
hear nothing.

188
00:08:44,965 --> 00:08:47,161
The small change that finally works.

189
00:08:47,229 --> 00:08:49,852
The numbers going up slowly, or not at
all.

190
00:08:49,934 --> 00:08:54,875
If you're thinking about starting something,
I want you to see that you can start

191
00:08:55,028 --> 00:08:56,187
with what you have.

192
00:08:56,223 --> 00:08:58,358
I'm not starting with a big budget.

193
00:08:58,424 --> 00:09:02,878
I'm starting with a plan, a tool I'm
building, and a list of real people.

194
00:09:03,016 --> 00:09:04,236
In the next episode,

195
00:09:07,796 --> 00:09:12,738
You can expect a long episode like this
regularly, with shorter clips in between,

196
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:15,575
built from whatever I actually did that
day.

197
00:09:15,658 --> 00:09:18,525
If a week is quiet, I'll tell you it was
quiet.

198
00:09:18,614 --> 00:09:22,518
If you want to watch whether this works,
or watch it fall apart,

199
00:09:22,639 --> 00:09:24,286
subscribe and follow along.

200
00:09:24,337 --> 00:09:25,863
I'll show you every step.

201
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:26,581
I'm Andrew.

202
00:09:26,602 --> 00:09:28,798
This is the Bootstrapper Experiment.

203
00:09:28,866 --> 00:09:30,269
Let's see what happens.

